.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
data/cache/
# DuckDB scratch from the feature engine. A stray `git add -A` committed 19 of
# these; they are regenerated on every run and belong to nobody.
.tmp/
data/raw/*
data/interim/*
data/processed/*
*.duckdb
.pytest_cache/
reports/*.png
# Generated artifacts that a stray `git add -A` swept in. The source they are
# built from is tracked; the output is not, for the same reason data/processed
# is not - it is reproducible, it is large, and a diff of it tells nobody
# anything. demo/address_index.json alone is 7MB and is rewritten by
# build_address_index.py on every retrain.
demo/address_index.json
demo/demo_data.json
demo/replay.json
demo/veridis_demo.html
reports/*.json
reports/*.md

# secrets
.env


# The ledger is why the repo is public: committed predictions carry GitHub's
# own commit timestamps, which is a far stronger anchor than "we published a
# page". So the ledger, and the few inputs needed to reproduce a prediction,
# are tracked - about 3MB - while the multi-gigabyte warehouse stays out.
#
# Note the `dir/*` form above rather than `dir/`: git does not descend into an
# excluded DIRECTORY, so a negation inside one never fires. Excluding the
# directory's CONTENTS leaves the path walkable and lets these re-include.
!data/ledger/
!data/ledger/*.jsonl
!data/processed/model_dest_latest.txt
# ledger_predict reads the shipped model's own banding threshold from here. It
# is 956 bytes and the nightly job cannot run without it - leaving it untracked
# crashed the ledger every night for four nights before anyone noticed, because
# a failed scheduled workflow is silent.
!data/processed/address_model_report.json
!data/processed/leadtime_roc.json
# The Ethereum side of the same bargain. eth_pit_report.json is the measured
# result, eth_holdout.json names the addresses it was measured on (so anyone can
# re-check a verdict against one the model never saw), and eth_parity_sample.json
# is what `make test` compares the browser's tree-walk against. 130KB for three
# claims that would otherwise have to be taken on trust.
!data/processed/eth_pit_report.json
!data/processed/eth_holdout.json
!data/processed/eth_parity_sample.json
# The two-sided Ethereum model's equivalents: the measured result, and the rows
# `make test` compares the browser's tree-walk against.
!data/processed/eth_pair_report.json
!data/processed/eth_pair_parity_sample.json
# The measured negative on native ETH, so the question is not re-opened on a hunch.
!data/processed/eth_native_report.json
!data/interim/scam_addresses.parquet
# Both freeze lists are tracked, and the Ethereum one has to be: the nightly
# refresh extends the stored list from its newest entry, so without a committed
# baseline CI would have nothing to extend and the Ethereum half of the ledger
# could never resolve. They are also the label source, which belongs in history.
!data/raw/tether_blacklist_tron.json
!data/raw/tether_blacklist_eth.json
# The out-of-sample check against freezes that post-date training. Kept so
# the claim can be re-read without refetching 163 histories.
!data/processed/fresh_freeze_report.json
# The payout_fanin probe: a measured negative, kept so it is not re-run on a hunch.
!data/processed/payout_fanin_probe.json
# The full payout fan-in result: a measured POSITIVE, and the measured reason
# it cannot be delivered by enlarging the warehouse.
!data/processed/payout_fanin_eval.json

# Build output - regenerated by `make dist`.
dist/

# Build input, not served. index_compact.json is what the page fetches and is
# derived from this; committing both would put 6.4MB of churn in history every
# time the index is rebuilt, for a file no browser requests.
site/address_index.json
.DS_Store
